D:\TOS\GD2

This directory contains "the new GEMDOS."  The differences from the old
GEMDOS are many, but also few.  There are no new calls.  Internal
management of handles is MUCH improved, so you don't get into trouble if
you are multitasking and you open the same file twice, dup and force
arbitrarily deeply, and stuff like that.  Directory scanning is tons
faster, so "rm *" in a directory with 300 files is REALLY FAST compared to
old GEMDOS.

This GEMDOS has been in use by Ken Bad and Jwahar Bammi (bammi@cadence.com)
since October 1991 or so.  They have used it under MiNT and pronounced
it good.  I haven't.

This GEMDOS is built with GCC.  Ken wants to rebuild it with Lattice;
that's fine with me.  In order to work with ALN, the assembly output from
GCC is transformed into Alcyon assembly code and assembled with MAS, the
Turbo C assembler.  The transformation is done with an Emacs script. Bammi
sent me a perl script that also does it, and possibly does it better, but
it's much slower.  The limited amount of variation in the assembly output
from the compiler makes this job pretty easy.

